    "prior_context": {
      "annotated_sentence_text": "[Turn 3 USER]\ne020\tOur aunt Leena is enthusiastic and has already offered to record narration.\ne021\tOur cousin Mara does not want a letter from her father displayed, even though Tomas says it explains why that branch of the family left.\ne022\tI agree with Mara about the letter.\ne023\tTomas says one person's discomfort should not control a shared history, but he has not said he would use it without permission.\ne024\tI catch myself treating his argument as a threat when it may be him thinking aloud.\n[Turn 3 ASSISTANT]\ne025\tIt is useful to separate the principle he is testing from the action he intends.\ne026\tMara's objection is specific and should be treated as real, while the broader question of who can speak for shared material remains unresolved.\ne027\tYou could exclude that letter and still acknowledge the missing part of the story, but doing so may shift the narrative in ways other relatives contest.\ne028\tConsent decisions do not automatically produce a neutral history; they define what kind of public project you are willing to make.",
